In PercentageCalculations.cs there are two different “percentage” style methods, and “weird results” usually happen when the wrong one is used or the inputs are interpreted incorrectly.

1) PercentageCalculations.PercentageOf(percentage, number)
This calculates a percentage of a number using (percentage / 100) * number.

Code (PercentageCalculations.cs, class PercentageCalculations, method PercentageOf):
public static double PercentageOf(double percentage, double number)
{
    return (percentage / 100) * number;
}

Important detail: percentage is expected as 25 for 25%, not 0.25.
So:
PercentageOf(25, 200) = 50
But if you pass 0.25:
PercentageOf(0.25, 200) = 0.5 (often perceived as “wrong/weird”)

2) PercentageCalculations.WhatPercentOf(part, whole)
This calculates what percent part is of whole using (part / whole) * 100, and throws if whole == 0.

Code (PercentageCalculations.cs, class PercentageCalculations, method WhatPercentOf):
public static double WhatPercentOf(double part, double whole)
{
    if (whole == 0)
    {
        throw new ArgumentException("The whole value cannot be zero");
    }
    return (part / whole) * 100;
}

So:
WhatPercentOf(25, 200) = 12.5
If you expected 50 here, you likely meant PercentageOf(25, 200).

