You can calculate period-to-period percentage change using PercentageCalculations.PercentageIncrease from PercentageCalculations.cs (class PercentageCalculations, method PercentageIncrease).

Relevant code (PercentageCalculations.cs, PercentageCalculations.PercentageIncrease):

public static double PercentageIncrease(double originalValue, double newValue)
{
    if (originalValue == 0)
    {
        throw new ArgumentException("Original value cannot be zero");
    }
    return ((newValue - originalValue) / originalValue) * 100;
}

How to apply it between each period
If you have a sequence of prices where each entry is a time period, compute the change from the previous period to the current period:

Example data:
double[] prices = { 100, 110, 105, 120 };

Period-to-period changes:
double[] pctChanges = new double[prices.Length - 1];

for (int i = 1; i < prices.Length; i++)
{
    pctChanges[i - 1] = PercentageCalculations.PercentageIncrease(prices[i - 1], prices[i]);
}

For the example above, pctChanges would be:
Period1->2: 10.0
Period2->3: -4.5454545...
Period3->4: 14.285714...

Important behavior details
1) This method returns positive for increases and negative for decreases, because it’s really “percentage change” despite the name PercentageIncrease (it uses (new - original) / original).
